  "title": "31‑metre Mary Celeste found adrift, crew and lifeboat gone",
  "description": "In December 1872, the brig Mary Celeste drifted off the Azores, its lifeboat gone and its cargo untouched. No one aboard was ever found, sparking endless speculation.\n\nKey points: the ship’s construction, its 1872 voyage, the discovery by the Dei Gratia, and the unresolved fate of its crew.\n\nBased on the Wikipedia article \"Mary Celeste\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Celeste), licensed CC BY-SA 4.0.\n\nImages from Wikimedia Commons:\nMary Celeste as Amazon in 1861 (cropped).jpg (Unconfirmed, possibly Honore Pellegrin (1800 – c.1870). This speculative attribu, Public domain): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mary_Celeste_as_Amazon_in_1861_(cropped).jpg\nMary Celeste NYTimes 1873February26.png (Boston Post, Public domain): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mary_Celeste_NYTimes_1873February26.png\nLap desk of Captain Benjamin Briggs from the Mary Celeste (PEM M6557) 02.jpg (Crawdad Blues, CC0): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lap_desk_of_Captain_Benjamin_Briggs_from_the_Mary_Celeste_(PEM_M6557)_02.jpg\nGonave.jpg (NASA, Public domain): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gonave.jpg\nArthur Conan Doyle by Herbert Rose Barraud 1893.jpg (Herbert Rose Barraud (1845 - c1896), Public domain): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_Conan_Doyle_by_Herbert_Rose_Barraud_1893.jpg",
